      "id": "xbscfwd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Classification of Rivers of India"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Himalayan rivers are perennial, snow-fed, and young, whereas Peninsular rivers are seasonal, rain-fed, and geologically older.",
        "The Ganga River originates as Bhagirathi from Gaumukh and officially becomes Ganga after joining Alaknanda at Devprayag.",
        "Brahmaputra is known as Yarlung Tsangpo in Tibet, Dihang in Arunachal Pradesh, and Jamuna in Bangladesh.",
        "Godavari, known as Dakshin Ganga or Vridha Ganga, forms the largest drainage basin in Peninsular India.",
        "Narmada and Tapi are major west-flowing rivers that flow through rift valleys into the Arabian Sea without forming deltas."
      ],
      "id": "e2hxaav"
    },
